    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.Welcome">Welcome aboard</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.WelcomeBody">WilcoATC gives you an air traffic controller that talks to you while you fly in Microsoft Flight Simulator. It listens on your COM radio, follows your flight, and answers when you call.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.WelcomePoints">• Everything runs on your machine — no account, no API key, no subscription.&#10;• Nothing you say leaves your computer.&#10;• This setup takes about two minutes. You can skip any step.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.Models">Speech models</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.ModelsBody">Two models do the work: one turns text into a controller's voice, the other turns your speech into text. Both are free and run offline.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.ModelAsr">Speech recognition (~460 MB)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.ModelVoice">Controller voice (~110 MB)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.ModelsSkip">You can skip this. Without them, WilcoATC still works: type your calls instead of speaking, and a built-in Windows voice replaces the controller.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.ModelsReady">Both models are installed. You are set.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.Download">Download both</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.Audio">Sound and microphone</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.AudioBody">Pick where the controller's voice comes out, and which microphone you talk into. Then test both — it is much easier to fix here than in flight.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.MicPrompt">Press Start, say “ready for departure”, then press Stop. The text you see is exactly what the ATC will hear.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.MicStart">Start recording</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.MicStop">Stop and transcribe</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.MicListening">Listening… speak now.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.MicTranscribing">Transcribing…</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.MicNothing">Nothing was heard. Check the microphone above and its Windows volume.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.MicNoModel">No recognition model installed — go back one step to download it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.Synthesising">Synthesising…</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.VoiceOk">Played. If you heard nothing, try another output device.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.VoiceNone">No voice available. Go back one step to download one.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.VoiceBusy">The radio channel was busy. Try again.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.PttPress">Press any key…</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.Flight">Talking and flight plans</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.FlightBody">Choose the key you hold to talk to ATC — it works even while the simulator has focus. If you use SimBrief, your username lets WilcoATC read your route, callsign and runways.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.Tour">Where things are</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.TourBody">Four things to know about the main window.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.TourPoints">• Frequencies — your COM1 and COM2, and the station WilcoATC matched to them.&#10;• Transmission log — everything said, by ATC, by you, and by the ambient traffic.&#10;• Speak — hold your push-to-talk key, or type a message and press Send.&#10;• Test Mode — accepts any request whatever the flight phase. Useful on the ground, to hear how it all sounds.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.Done">You are ready</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.DoneBody">Start a flight, tune your COM1 to an airport frequency, and the controller will call you. Everything below can be changed later in Settings.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.SummaryNoVoice">none — a Windows voice will be used</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.SummaryNoAsr">none — type your calls instead</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.SummaryNoPtt">not set</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.Next">Next</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.Back">Back</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.Finish">Finish</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.SkipAll">Skip setup</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.Relaunch">Setup assistant</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.RelaunchHint">Run the first-launch assistant again — models, sound, microphone, push-to-talk.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ob.RelaunchBtn">Run setup again</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="S.Radio.Samples">Real radio samples</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Radio.SamplesHint">Drop .wav files in the folder below to replace the synthesised clicks with real ones. The name prefix picks the category: keyup* (PTT press), breath* (pilot breathing in), tail* (squelch tail), bed* (transmitting cockpit noise), background* (continuous VHF hiss under the voice). Several files per category are picked at random, so no two calls sound alike. Breath and bed have no synthesised fallback — without a file, nothing is played; background* replaces the synthesised hiss when present.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Radio.OpenFolder">Open samples folder</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Radio.Rescan">Rescan</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Radio.BedVolume">Transmitting cockpit noise</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Radio.BedVolumeHint">How loud the caller's own background sits under their voice. It cuts dead when they release the key — that abrupt stop is a large part of what makes a transmission believable.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="S.Audio.Intensity">Radio effect strength</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Audio.IntensityHint">Left: nearly clean, the voice keeps its timbre. Right: heavy VHF colouring. Bandwidth, hiss and saturation all follow this one slider.</sys:String>

    <!-- ===== Settings — ATC ===== -->
    <sys:String x:Key="S.AtcTab.Title">ATC behavior</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.AtcTab.Subtitle">Automatic contact, GSX integration and test mode.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Atc.Delay">Response delay</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Atc.DelayHint">Pause before each transmission — the controller's thinking time. It runs AT THE SAME TIME as speech synthesis, so a reply costs the longer of the two, never their sum. Lower it for a snappy controller, raise it for a busy one. 0 = speaks as soon as the voice is ready.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Atc.Fighter">Send a fighter (MSFS 2024)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Atc.FighterHint">After the radio-failure call, a fighter is CREATED in the simulator and flies formation off your left wing, then leaves. This is the only feature that writes into your sim world, hence off by default. The F/A-18E from MSFS 2024 is used automatically; nothing is created if the aircraft is missing.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Atc.FighterTitle">Aircraft title (blank = auto)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Atc.FighterDuration">Escort duration</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Atc.Intercept">Radio failure call</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Atc.InterceptHint">Three calls with no answer and the controller concludes you have lost your radio: he asks for squawk 7600, tells you to continue as filed, and stops calling. A fighter only shows up — and only speaks — if you turn on the option below.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Atc.Readback">Mandatory readback</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Atc.ReadbackHint">After a clearance, the controller asks for a read-back if you stay silent (two prompts, then he moves on), and rejects an incomplete one: a bare “roger” does not count as reading back a runway, a squawk, a frequency or an altitude — he repeats the instruction until you do.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Atc.Language">Controller language</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Atc.LanguageHint">Auto = the language of the country you are flying in, read from the airport's ICAO prefix (LF = French, ED = German, LE = Spanish, LI = Italian…), and the controller switches to yours as soon as you speak to them. He UNDERSTANDS every supported language whatever this setting says — speech recognition is multilingual. Everywhere else on the globe, control is in English, as it is in real life.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.AtcTab.AutoContact">Automatic initial contact</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.AtcTab.AutoContactHint">When you tune to a known station's frequency, ATC contacts you automatically.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.AtcTab.Atis">ATIS broadcast</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.AtcTab.AtisHint">Tune an airport's ATIS frequency and a voice reads the field bulletin on a loop — runway in use, wind, visibility, temperature and QNH, taken from the simulator's own weather. Leave the frequency and it stops.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.AtcTab.AtisGap">Gap between repeats</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.AtcTab.FlightRules">Flight rules</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.AtcTab.FlightRulesHint">Auto reads VFR or IFR from your flight plan, and otherwise from the aircraft — a light piston flies VFR. Force the choice if the detection gets it wrong.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.AtcTab.Gsx">Trigger GSX on pushback</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.AtcTab.GsxHint">On pushback clearance, activate GSX auto-pushback (via beacon + parking brake).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.AtcTab.TestMode">🧪 Test Mode</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.AtcTab.TestModeHint">ON = every request is GRANTED regardless of phase or frequency, and the “departed without clearance” warning is suppressed. The state machine and rules stay intact (just bypassed). An orange banner shows in the main window while it is active.</sys:String>

    <!-- ===== Settings — Handoffs / Center ===== -->
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tr.Title">Frequency handoffs / Center</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tr.Subtitle">Tower / Ground / Departure / Approach frequencies come from OurAirports data. Center (en-route) has no reliable offline source.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tr.Sectors">En-route sectors (Center frequencies)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tr.SectorsHint">Center is a SECTOR service: its frequencies belong to no airport, which is why airport data publishes none outside North America — and why the controller keeps you on Departure for the whole cruise. Installing the sectors gives the real frequency and the real name (“Bordeaux Control”) for the piece of sky you are actually in. Downloaded on demand, ~3 MB, then fully offline.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tr.SectorsBtn">⤓  Install en-route sectors (~3 MB)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tr.Vatsim">VATSIM: real Center frequency if online</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tr.VatsimHint">If enabled and the controller is online on VATSIM, the real Center frequency is used.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tr.CenterName">Center name</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tr.CenterNameHint">E.g. “Geneva” → “Geneva Center”. Filling this field ENABLES the Center handoff with the approximate frequency below (otherwise the Center handoff is skipped).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tr.CenterFreq">Approximate Center frequency (MHz)</sys:String>

